WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.500
What is the smallest positive whole number that you can

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:06.190
multiply ninety by to make a perfect square?

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:09.610
Try to think of ninety as a collection of prime pieces.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.400
A square will let those pieces split into

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:13.710
two identical groups.

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:20.270
Ninety factorises into two times three times

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:21.380
three times five.

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:24.560
Each prime needs an even number of copies in a square.

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:26.720
The threes already make a pair.

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:29.340
The two and the five each appear once.

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:32.120
Put a space for a partner beside each of them.

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:35.220
Supply one more two and one more five.

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:36.760
Those are the missing tiles.

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:41.090
Their product is ten, so multiplying ninety by ten adds

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:42.740
just the two partners we need.

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:45.140
Now each prime has an even count.

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:48.100
Ninety times ten is nine hundred.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:52.070
Split its tiles into two identical groups, each with a

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:53.840
two, a three and a five.

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:56.120
Each group multiplies to thirty.

00:00:56.750 --> 00:01:00.330
Thirty times thirty is nine hundred, so the new number

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:01.080
is a square.

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:04.220
Could a smaller positive multiplier work?

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:07.770
Any multiplier that works must supply a two and a five,

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:09.780
because those counts start odd.

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:13.390
A number divisible by the two distinct primes two and

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:15.060
five is a multiple of ten.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:18.230
Ten is the first positive multiple and we have

00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:19.040
shown it works.

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:22.340
For a square, pair every prime.

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:25.800
Choose one partner for each prime whose count is odd.

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:27.780
Try the same proof with sixty.

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:30.910
Find the smallest positive whole number to multiply

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:34.650
sixty by to make a square, and check your answer by

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:37.580
splitting the new prime tiles into two equal groups.
